# Day 13 – The TCP/IP Model

## Objective

Understand the TCP/IP Model, its four layers, how it compares to the OSI Model, and why it is the standard model used on modern networks.

---

# What is the TCP/IP Model?

The TCP/IP (Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol) Model is the networking model used on the Internet.

Unlike the OSI Model, which has seven layers, the TCP/IP Model has only four layers.

It defines how devices communicate over networks and how data is transmitted from one device to another.

---

# Why is the TCP/IP Model Important?

The TCP/IP Model:

- Powers the Internet.
- Defines communication standards.
- Ensures devices from different manufacturers can communicate.
- Forms the foundation of modern networking.

---

# The Four Layers of the TCP/IP Model

## 1. Application Layer

The Application Layer combines the functions of the OSI Application, Presentation, and Session layers.

Responsibilities:
- User interaction
- Data formatting
- Encryption
- Communication sessions

Common Protocols:
- HTTP
- HTTPS
- FTP
- SMTP
- DNS
- SSH

---

## 2. Transport Layer

Responsible for end-to-end communication between devices.

Functions:
- Segmentation
- Error checking
- Flow control
- Reliable data delivery

Protocols:
- TCP
- UDP

### TCP

Characteristics:
- Reliable
- Connection-oriented
- Guarantees delivery

Examples:
- Banking websites
- Email
- File downloads

---

### UDP

Characteristics:
- Faster
- Connectionless
- No delivery guarantee

Examples:
- Online gaming
- Video streaming
- Voice calls

---

## 3. Internet Layer

Responsible for routing packets between different networks.

Functions:
- Logical addressing
- Routing
- Packet forwarding

Protocols:
- IP
- ICMP

Device:
- Router

---

## 4. Network Access Layer

Responsible for transmitting data across the physical network.

Functions:
- Physical addressing
- Error detection
- Physical transmission

Devices:
- Switch
- NIC
- Ethernet cables
- Wi-Fi

---

# TCP/IP Model vs OSI Model

| TCP/IP Model | OSI Model |
|--------------|-----------|
| Application | Application, Presentation, Session |
| Transport | Transport |
| Internet | Network |
| Network Access | Data Link + Physical |

---

# Encapsulation

As data moves down the TCP/IP Model:

Application Data

↓

Segments

↓

Packets

↓

Frames

↓

Bits

Each layer adds its own header before sending the data to the next layer.

---

# Decapsulation

When data reaches the destination:

- The Network Access Layer removes its header.
- The Internet Layer removes its header.
- The Transport Layer removes its header.
- The Application Layer presents the original data to the user.

---

# Why This Matters in Cybersecurity

Cybersecurity professionals use the TCP/IP Model to:

- Analyze network traffic.
- Investigate cyber attacks.
- Understand packet flow.
- Configure firewalls.
- Troubleshoot communication problems.

Knowing which protocol operates at which layer helps identify where attacks occur.

---

# Key Takeaways

- The TCP/IP Model has four layers.
- It is the networking model used on the Internet.
- The Application Layer combines three OSI layers.
- TCP provides reliable communication.
- UDP prioritizes speed over reliability.
- Routers operate at the Internet Layer.
- Switches operate at the Network Access Layer.
